#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Actively re-read an EmotivPRO recording at higher fidelity than the
live multi-channel view `epoc-record` normally reads, by driving
EmotivPRO's own replay UI (via simulated xdotool clicks) to isolate one
electrode or motion sensor at a time.

Given an `epoc-record` output log (plain or gzipped CSV, e.g.
``log.20260814-1659.gz``), this script:

1. Reads the log's overall time span and, per channel, its observed
   value range (used to plan zoom levels -- see below).
2. Finds the EmotivPRO window, navigates to the Recordings list, OCRs
   each row's "Date Collected"/"Duration" cell, and picks the recording
   whose time span overlaps the log's -- since `epoc-record`'s own
   timestamps are only approximate (screen-OCR derived), this uses a
   padded-overlap match, not exact equality.
3. Opens that recording. For each electrode (and, with --motion-too,
   each motion sensor), for each zoom level (EEG only) x high-pass
   filter state (EEG only): rewinds to the start, isolates that one
   channel via the eye/"Show or hide channel" panel, sets the Amplitude
   min/max fields (EEG) or leaves it autoscaled (Motion, which has no
   such fields at all), plays the recording through, and digitizes the
   single visible curve column-by-column exactly like `epoc-record`
   digitizes each of its 14 simultaneous curves -- reusing the same
   plot-bounds/OCR/stitching machinery from ``epoc_graph.py``, just with
   a single-curve-specific ink detector instead of per-channel color
   disambiguation (irrelevant here since only one curve is ever on
   screen during a reread pass).
4. Writes each pass to its own incrementally-flushed CSV, and appends a
   short cross-correlation-based "common timespan" report comparing the
   freshly re-read series against the original log's (imprecise) same-
   channel column.

Y-axis precision: --realtime-budget
------------------------------------
Rather than picking an abstract "number of zoom levels," you say how many
multiples of the recording's OWN duration you're willing to spend
re-reading each electrode (``--realtime-budget K``, default 2). That
budget is spent in priority order, 1x each, stopping when it runs out:

1. **fine**: a tight range covering the log's own 1st-99th percentile of
   observed (non-blank) values for that channel, padded ~10% -- the
   highest-resolution read of the "normal" signal, and the most valuable
   single read if the budget only stretches to one.
2. **wide**: EmotivPRO's "Autoscale" button turned out (confirmed live)
   to just reset the Amplitude fields back to the fixed +/-100uV default,
   NOT a true data-driven autofit -- so it is USELESS for finding true
   extremes and is not used here. Instead the wide pass uses a generous
   multiple of the log's own observed |value| max for that channel
   (which is only a LOWER bound on the truth, since a value that went
   off-screen during the original `epoc-record` capture -- clipped by
   its fixed +/-100uV default -- would show up as a blank/missing sample,
   not a wrong one). If the re-read data still touches this pass's own
   boundary, a warning is written to the match-report recommending a
   larger --wide-factor and a re-run.
3. Any further budget (K > 2) buys extra levels geometrically
   interpolated between the fine and wide ranges.

``--with-high-pass`` re-reads every level --realtime-budget selected a
second time with EmotivPRO's High-pass filter on -- a separate opt-in
multiplier (doubles the time spent) rather than part of the budget,
since it's a different signal (filtered vs raw), not a precision tier.

Motion sensors (--motion-too) have neither an Amplitude control nor a
High-pass filter checkbox in EmotivPRO's UI (confirmed live) -- they get
exactly one pass each, autoscaled, whatever range EmotivPRO happens to
render, unaffected by --realtime-budget/--with-high-pass.

This script is NOT passive: unlike `epoc-record`, it sends real
mouse/keyboard input to the EmotivPRO window (via `xdotool`). Do not run
it during a live/currently-recording session -- it operates the
Recordings list and a recording's replay view, not live capture.

**Must be run attended, in the foreground -- never backgrounded/detached
(no `&`, `nohup`, etc).** It shares the mouse/keyboard/window with
whatever else is happening on the desktop, and it has no way to tell the
difference between "EmotivPRO redrew because of something I clicked" and
"the user just moved the mouse" except by checking for it: before every
click and periodically during a pass, it verifies the EmotivPRO window
is still the same size, still focused, and the pointer is still where it
last put it. The instant one of those checks fails, it stops clicking,
prints what it saw, and blocks on stdin for you to confirm the UI is
idle again before it resumes -- from the start of the current pass, not
mid-action, since there's no way to know what state a human left things
in. (Confirmed live why an independent duration matters: an early
version's end-of-playback detection was icon-based and had two different
real bugs at different times -- once truncating every pass of a 66-pass
run to ~10s, later silently dropping the last ~10% of every pass. It's
scrub-handle-position-based now (see MIN_PLAYING_BEFORE_STOP_S), but give
it a known --duration whenever you have one regardless; that's a hard
floor no misdetection of any kind can undercut.)

Runtime is long: each pass takes as long as the recording itself (replay
is periodically resynced against the scrub handle to correct for actual
playback speed, confirmed live to NOT be a reliable 1x over a long
pass). For N electrodes, a
--realtime-budget of K, an H multiplier (2 if --with-high-pass else 1),
and a recording of duration D, EEG alone takes roughly N*K*H*D. Use
--channels/--realtime-budget/--dry-run to scope a test run.
"""

